# DESCRIPTION

mali drives an Arm Mali GPU of the command-stream (CSF) generations, so far only as far as a first probe: it powers the GPU up and reads what it is.
devmgr(8) starts it for the CIX Sky1's GPU:

```text
driver mali
class gpu
match acpi CIXH5000
own-mmu
coherence noncoherent CIX runs it on ACE-Lite, uncached (AbyssBSD gpu.md)
power-up graph
power-clocks gpu_clk_core gpu_clk_stacks
reset-pulse 20
firmware arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin
```

The GPU translates through page tables mali writes, so `own-mmu` gives it the BTI of a device behind no IOMMU whatever the IORT names, and the kernel warns of it.
Its `_CCA` says coherent and the GPU isn't, as CIX's own kernel treats it (the display controller doesn't snoop), so the record's `coherence` wins and mali is started with `--noncoherent`.
`vx hardware trust cix-sky1` lists it as trusted with all of memory.

The Sky1's GPU answers nothing until its power-up is done, in this order, and a read of its registers before it stops the machine (an SError, then a power cycle).
devmgr(8) does the first four steps before it starts the driver, since the record says `power-up graph` (driver(6)), each through what owns it:

1. its power domain, SCMI domain 21 (`_DSD`'s `power-domains`), turned on through scmi(3);
2. its clocks `gpu_clk_core` and `gpu_clk_stacks` (`CLKT`: SCMI ids 31 and 32) turned on the same way;
3. its power resource's `_ON`, which devmgr runs;
4. its reset, `gpu_reset` in `RSTL` (index 9 on the S5 reset controller), pulsed through the controller's driver, sky1-reset(3): held for 20 µs and let go, and given 20 µs more (the record's `reset-pulse 20`: the GPU needs the line asserted and let go, not only released).

The driver starts with the GPU on and does the one step that is in the GPU's own registers:

5. Q-channel clock gating turned on, bit 0 of its RCSU's (its first range's) register `0x218`.

A step of the driver's that fails stops it there, and no GPU register is read; a step of devmgr's that fails keeps the driver from starting (`failed: power-up` in devmgr's `status`, and the step in its `power`).
Then it reads, from its second range, GPU_ID, the L2, tiler, memory and MMU features, the address spaces, the CSF id, GPU_FEATURES and the shader core, tiler and L2 present masks, and names the GPU from them as Arm does (`Mali-G720-Immortalis`: architecture 12, product 0, ten cores or more, with ray intersection).
A GPU_ID of 0 or all ones, or before the 10th architecture, is no GPU.

Its record names the CSF firmware, `firmware arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin` (driver(6), firmware(6)), so devmgr gives its host that one file at `/lib/firmware/arm/mali/arch12.8/mali_csffw.bin`, read only.
On start mali reads it and checks its header and every section against the format's rules, and reports how many sections it holds and the revision it was built from, or the rule it breaks, without loading it into the GPU; where the image has no firmware (the cache was empty when it was built), it says `no firmware`.

The GPU is left powered, and its job, MMU and GPU interrupts unused.

# THE CSF CAPSET

mali is a GPU class driver (class `gpu`): devmgr(8) mounts its tree at `/dev/gpu<n>`, and its `gpu` service is the class's Device (gpu-device-device(5)), which offers one capset, `csf` (0x1000).
`open` on it returns a channel that speaks `todhchai.gpu.csf.Context` (gpu-csf-context(5)), one context to a client, closed with the channel: a context's groups end and its VMs, buffers and counters go.
The objects and their limits are panthor's, the Linux driver's, without its ioctls:

: VM
  A GPU address space: 32 to a context, handles 1 to 32.
  The client owns the addresses below the `userRange` `createVM` returns, and the driver keeps the rest for its rings and sync objects.
: Buffer
  Pinned write-combining memory, up to 4 GiB, and 4 GiB all together in a context.
  The client maps its VMO; the driver binds ranges of it into a VM (4 KiB pages, 2 MiB where aligned; read-only, no-execute and uncached flags).
  A bind replaces what it overlaps, splitting it.
  A buffer let go of stays until nothing maps it.
: Group
  1 to 32 queues (and no more than the firmware's command-stream slots) over one VM; 128 to a context, handles 1 to 128.
  Each queue has a ring of 4 KiB to 64 KiB that only the driver writes.
  A group's priority is 0 or 1: high and realtime are refused, since no context holds the privilege.
  More groups than the firmware has slots run in turn.
: Submission
  Jobs, each a queue and a stream's address and size (64-byte aligned, a multiple of 8 bytes) in the VM, or no stream for a sync point.
  The driver writes the ring's sequence around the stream: a cache flush, the call, and a SYNC_ADD64 of the queue's sync object.
  A submission can wait on the client's counters, which it keeps in order with the group's other submissions, and raises its signals when its last job is done, whether the job ended well or not.

A group ends when a command stream faults fatally, the GPU's MMU faults on its VM, a job runs past 5 seconds, or a reset ends it: its jobs are cancelled and their signals raised, `groupState` says why, and the context's `submit` and `createGroup` fail with `lost`.
The context's other groups, and every other context, go on.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/mali-acpi-_SB_.GPU_`.

: `status`
  Read only.
  The firmware first: `firmware <size> bytes, <n> sections, git <revision>, not loaded` (no revision when the image has none), `firmware <size> bytes refused: <rule>, not loaded`, or `no firmware`.
  Then the step done: `Q-channel clock gating on, RCSU control <hex> harvest <hex>`.
  Then what was read: the name, GPU_ID and its fields (`Mali-G720-Immortalis, GPU_ID 0xc8700008: architecture 12.8.7, r0p0 status 8`), the cores (`shader cores 10 (0x00550555), tiler 0x00000001, L2 0x00000001`), the feature registers, and `probed`.
  Where a step failed, `FAILED at <step>: <why>`, the step `Q-channel clock gating` or `probe`.

